---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="How to Scan a QR Code on iPhone (2026 Guide)"
    description="Learn how to scan QR codes on iPhone using the Camera app, Control Center, or Photos app. Includes troubleshooting tips, safety warnings, and a step-by-step guide for iOS 17 and iOS 18."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 5, 2026 · Updated: October 8, 2026
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                How to Scan a QR Code on iPhone (2026 Guide)
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                You don't need a third-party app to scan QR codes on iPhone. iOS
                has built-in scanners that work in seconds. Here are three ways
                to use them — plus troubleshooting fixes for when they don't
                work.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                I remember the first time I tried to scan a QR code on my
                iPhone. It was at a café, and the menu was behind a small square
                sticker on the table. I pointed my camera at it, waited, and
                nothing happened. I tried again. Nothing. I ended up asking the
                waiter for a paper menu — feeling slightly defeated.
            </p>
            <p>
                Later that day, I learned what I was doing wrong: the phone was
                too close. Once I moved it back a few centimeters, the code
                scanned instantly. Since then, I've figured out exactly how
                iPhone QR scanning works — and where it quietly fails. This
                guide covers everything.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: How to Scan a QR Code on iPhone
            </h2>
            <p>
                Open the Camera app, point it at the QR code, and wait 1–2
                seconds. A yellow banner appears at the top of the screen — tap
                it to open the link. No app installation needed. iOS has
                supported this since iOS 11.
            </p>
            <p>
                But there are two other methods that are faster or more
                convenient in specific situations. Let's go through all three.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 1: Camera App (Simplest)
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is the default method and works on every iPhone from the
                iPhone 6s onwards.
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open the <strong>Camera</strong> app.</li>
                <li>Point your camera at the QR code.</li>
                <li>Hold steady for 1–2 seconds.</li>
                <li>
                    A yellow notification banner appears at the top of the
                    screen.
                </li>
                <li>Tap the banner to open the code's link or content.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <strong>Key detail most guides miss:</strong> The QR code should fill
                about 60–70% of your screen. If it's too small in the frame, the camera
                won't detect it. If it's too large, the edges get cut off and it also
                won't detect.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 2: Control Center (Fastest for Frequent Scanners)
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you scan QR codes regularly — for work, business cards, or
                events — adding the Code Scanner to Control Center saves real
                time. It opens the scanner directly instead of going through the
                Camera app.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                How to Add Code Scanner to Control Center:
            </h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
                <li>Tap <strong>Control Center</strong>.</li>
                <li>
                    Scroll to find <strong>Code Scanner</strong> in the "More Controls"
                    list.
                </li>
                <li>Tap the green <strong>+</strong> button next to it.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">How to Use It:</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Swipe down from the top-right corner to open Control Center.
                </li>
                <li>Tap the <strong>Code Scanner</strong> icon.</li>
                <li>Point at the QR code — it scans automatically.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                I added this to my Control Center two years ago and haven't gone
                back. It's especially useful when scanning multiple codes
                quickly.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 3: Photos App (For Screenshots and Images)
            </h2>
            <p>
                If someone sent you a screenshot of a QR code, or you saved one
                from the web, you don't need to scan it from another screen. iOS
                can read it directly from your Photos library.
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open the <strong>Photos</strong> app.</li>
                <li>Tap the image containing the QR code.</li>
                <li>Tap and hold on the QR code.</li>
                <li>A menu appears showing the code's link or content.</li>
                <li>Tap it to open.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                This uses Apple's <strong>Live Text</strong> feature, available on
                iOS 15 and later. It works for QR codes, text, phone numbers, and
                even addresses in photos.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Comparison: Which Method Should You Use?
            </h2>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Method</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Best For</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Time</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Camera app</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >One-off scans, quick checks</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">2–3 sec</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Control Center</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Frequent scanners (work, events)</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">1–2 sec</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Photos app</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Screenshots, saved images</td>
                            <td class="p-3">3–4 sec</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Why Your iPhone Won't Scan a QR Code (Troubleshooting)
            </h2>
            <p>
                If the Camera app isn't detecting a QR code, try these fixes in
                order. I've used all of them personally at some point.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                1. Clean Your Camera Lens
            </h3>
            <p>
                This is the #1 reason iPhone cameras fail to scan QR codes. A
                thin layer of fingerprint oil on the lens scatters light and
                confuses the scanner. Wipe the lens with a soft cloth — the
                difference is often immediate.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">2. Fix the Lighting</h3>
            <p>
                QR codes need <strong>even lighting</strong>, not bright glare.
                Direct sunlight or a strong overhead lamp can create reflections
                that break the pattern. If you're outdoors, try moving into
                shade.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                3. Adjust the Distance
            </h3>
            <p>
                As I learned at that café: QR codes need to fill about 60% of
                the frame. If yours isn't scanning, try moving the phone <strong
                    >10–15 cm back</strong
                > or
                <strong>slightly forward</strong>.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">4. Hold Steady</h3>
            <p>
                The Camera app needs 1–2 seconds to detect a QR code. If your
                hands are shaky (walking, moving vehicle), the camera can't lock
                on. Stop moving, hold with both hands, and wait.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                5. Check Camera Settings
            </h3>
            <p>In rare cases, QR scanning gets disabled in Settings:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Open <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>Camera</strong>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Look for <strong>"Scan QR Codes"</strong> and make sure it's toggled
                    on.
                </li>
                <li>If not, turn it on and try again.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                6. Restart Your iPhone
            </h3>
            <p>
                If none of the above works, restart the phone. It's cliché
                advice, but it fixes glitchy camera behavior more often than
                you'd expect.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                ⚠️ QR Code Safety: What to Watch For
            </h2>
            <p>
                QR codes themselves are safe — they're just encoded data. The
                risk is where they lead. Here's what I check before scanning an
                unfamiliar code:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Look at the URL preview.</strong> iOS shows the destination
                    URL before opening. If it looks suspicious (typos, strange domains),
                    don't tap.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Beware of stickers on top of QR codes.</strong> A common
                    scam is pasting a fake QR code sticker over a real one — especially
                    on parking meters and payment terminals.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Never enter passwords or card details.</strong> No legitimate
                    QR code should ask for sensitive info on a random page.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Trust the source.</strong> Codes from banks, restaurants,
                    and official packaging are generally safe. Codes on random posters
                    or emails are not.
                </li>
            </ul>

