                <a
                    href="/blog/what-is-qr-code"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    what QR codes are and how they work
                </a>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Do I need to install an app to scan QR codes?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. iPhone cameras have had built-in QR scanning since
                        iOS 11 (2017). All you need is the default Camera app.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Why does the yellow banner disappear before I can tap
                        it?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        The banner disappears when the QR code leaves the camera
                        frame. Keep the camera pointed at the code until you've
                        tapped the banner.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I scan a QR code from a screenshot?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. Open the screenshot in Photos, tap and hold on the
                        QR code, and iOS will detect it. This works on iOS 15
                        and later.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Does scanning a QR code cost data?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        The scan itself doesn't use data. But if the QR code
                        opens a website, that site loads using your mobile data
                        or Wi-Fi.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What if my iPhone model is older?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        QR scanning works on iPhone 6s and later running iOS
                        11+. If your phone is older than that, you'd need a
                        third-party app — but that's extremely rare in 2026.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I scan QR codes with Siri?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. Siri can't scan QR codes. You'll need to use the
                        Camera app, Control Center, or Photos app.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                Scanning QR codes on iPhone is fast, free, and built-in. The
                Camera app handles almost every situation, Control Center is
                faster for frequent use, and Photos handles saved images. Once
                you know the three methods and the common troubleshooting fixes,
                you'll never be stuck again.
            </p>
            <p>
                And if you need to{" "}
                <a
